\id AMO - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.14
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Amos
\toc1 Amos
\toc2 Amos
\mt1 Amos
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This is\add* the message that Amos (who was among the shepherds in Tekoa) saw concerning Israel in the days of King Uzziah of Judah, and also in the days of Yoash’s son King Jeroboam of Israel, two years before the earthquake.
\q1
\v 2 Amos said,
\q1 “Yahweh will shout loudly from Zion.
\q1 He will raise his voice from Jerusalem.
\q1 The shepherds’ pastures will brown off.
\q1 The top of \add Mt.\add* Carmel will dry up.”
\s1 Judgement of Israel’s neighbours
\s2 Syria
\p
\v 3 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Damascus was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away \add their\add* punishment,
\q1 for the cruel things they did to the people of Gilead.
\q1
\v 4 I’ll send a fire into \add King\add* Hazael’s house,
\q1 and it will devour \add King\add* Ben-Hadad’s fortresses.
\q1
\v 5 I’ll smash the gate bars of Damascus
\q1 and eliminate the ruler of the Aven valley,
\q1 and also the leader of Beyt-Eden.
\q1 The people of Syria will go in captivity to Kir,”
\mi says Yahweh.
\s2 Philistia
\p
\v 6 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Gaza was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away \add their\add* punishment,
\q1 because they carried a whole people group away as captives,
\q1 to hand them over to Edom.
\v 7 I’ll send fire onto Gaza’s walls,
\q1 and it’ll devour her fortresses.
\v 8 I will cut off those from Ashdod
\q1 and the ruler of Ashkelon.
\q1 I’ll cause problems for Ekron,
\q1 and the rest of the Philistines will die,”
\q1 says Yahweh the master.
\s2 Tyre
\p
\v 9 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Tyre was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away \add their\add* punishment,
\q1 because they handed a whole people group over to Edom,
\q1 and they didn’t take any notice of their brotherhood agreement.
\q1
\v 10 I’ll send fire onto Tyre’s walls,
\q1 and it’ll devour her fortresses.”
\s2 Edom
\p
\v 11 This is what Yahweh says,
\q1 “Even if Edom was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away \add their\add* punishment,
\q1 because he pursued his brother with the sword and abandoned all pity.
\q1 His anger raged continually,
\q1 and his rage lasted forever.
\q1
\v 12 I’ll send fire on Teyman,
\q1 and it’ll devour Batserah’s fortresses.”
\s2 Amon
\p
\v 13 This is what Yahweh says,
\q1 “Even if the Ammonites were three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away \add their\add* punishment,
\q1 because they ripped open the pregnant women of Gilead,
\q1 in order to grab territory.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll light a fire in Rabah’s walls,
\q1 and it’ll devour its fortresses,
\q1 with a shout in the day of battle,
\q1 with a storm in the day of the whirlwind.
\q1
\v 15 Their king will go into captivity along with his officials,”
\mi says Yahweh.
\c 2
\s2 Moab
\p
\v 1 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Moab was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away its punishment,
\q1 because he completely burnt up the bones of Edom’s king.
\q1
\v 2 I’ll send fire on Moab,
\q1 and it will devour Keriyot’s fortresses.
\q1 Moab will die in an uproar—
\q1 with shouting and the sound of the horn.
\v 3 I’ll wipe out her judge,
\q1 and I’ll kill all the leaders along with him,”
\mi says Yahweh.
\s1 Judah’s punishment
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 4 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Judah was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away its punishment,
\q1 because they rejected Yahweh’s instructions
\q1 and didn’t keep his regulations.
\q1 Their lies caused them to go astray,
\q1 just like their ancestors had done.
\v 5 I’ll send fire on Judah,
\q1 and it’ll devour Jerusalem’s fortresses.”
\s1 Israel’s punishment
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 6 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Even if Israel was three or four sins below the threshold,
\q1 I wouldn’t turn away its punishment,
\q1 because they sold the innocent for silver
\q1 and the needy for a pair of sandals.
\v 7 They trample the heads of the poor into the dust.
\q1 They \add just\add* push the oppressed out of the way.
\q1 A man and his father go lie with the same girl
\q1 →and so profane my sinless reputation.
\v 8 They lie down beside every altar on clothes taken \add from the poor\add* as pledges,
\q1 and in the house of their god they drink the wine of those who were fined.
\p
\v 9 Yet I destroyed the Amorites before them.
\q1 They were as tall as of cedars
\q1 \add and\add* as strong as oaks.
\q1 ^Yet I destroyed their fruit \add up\add* high
\q1 and their roots \add down\add* below.
\v 10 Also, I brought you all out of Egypt
\q1 and led you forty years in the wilderness
\q1 so you all could possess the land of the Amorites.
\q1
\v 11 I raised prophets up from among your sons
\q1 and \add devoted\add* Nazirites from your young men.
\q1 Isn’t that right, you Israelites?
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 12 But you all persuaded the Nazirites to drink wine
\q1 and ordered the prophets not to prophesy.
\q1
\v 13 Listen, I’m going to make you lose your stability
\q1 just like a cart wobbles when it’s carrying a \add heavy\add* load of grain.
\q1
\v 14 Even fast people won’t be able to reach a safe place.
\q1 The strong won’t have enough strength of their own,
\q1 and the powerful won’t be able to save themselves.
\q1
\v 15 The \add archer\add* holding the bow will be forced to retreat.
\q1 \add Even\add* the fast runner won’t be able to escape.
\q1 The horse-rider won’t escape with his life.
\q1
\v 16 Even the bravest warriors will flee naked that day.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Hear this message that Yahweh has spoken against you, people of Israel, against Jacob’s descendants that I brought out of Egypt saying:
\v 2 “I have chosen only you from all the families of the earth, therefore I’ll punish you all for all your sins.”
\s1 Cause and effect
\s4 Northern kingdom
\q1
\v 3 Do two people walk together, without first agreeing to meet?
\q1
\v 4 Would a lion roar in the forest when he doesn’t have any prey?
\q1 Would a young lion growl from his den if he’s caught nothing?
\q1
\v 5 Would a bird fall into a trap on the ground when no bait has been put in it?
\q1 Would a trap \add just\add* spring up from the ground when it hasn’t caught anything?
\q1
\v 6 If a \add warning\add* trumpet sounds in a city, won’t the people start to worry?
\q1 If disaster comes to a city, wouldn’t it be Yahweh who sent it?
\q1
\v 7 Surely the master Yahweh wouldn’t do anything unless he \add first\add* reveals his plan to his servants the prophets.
\q1
\v 8 The lion has roared—who won’t be afraid?
\q1 The master Yahweh has spoken—who won’t prophesy?
\s1 The punishment of Samaria
\s4 Northern kingdom
\q1
\v 9 Declare this in the fortresses at Ashdod,
\q1 and those in Egypt,
\q1 saying, “Assemble yourselves on Samaria’s mountains
\q1 and see what great confusion is in her,
\q1 and what oppression is in her.
\q1
\v 10 Yahweh declares that they don’t know how to do right.
\q1 They store up violence and destruction in their fortresses.”
\p
\v 11 Therefore, this is what the master Yahweh says:
\q1 “An enemy will surround the land,
\q1 and weaken your powerful \add grip\add*,
\q1 →and \add then\add* your fortresses will be looted.”
\p
\v 12 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “\add Just\add* as the shepherd rescues out of the lion’s mouth two legs only, or a piece of an ear,
\q1 so will the Israelites who live in Samaria be rescued with only the corner of a couch or a piece of a bed.”
\v 13 Take notice \add of this\add* and testify against Jacob’s descendants.
\mi That is the declaration of the master Yahweh, Commander-in-chief God.
\p
\v 14 “On the day that I bring punishment for Israel’s sins to her, I’ll also bring punishment on the altars of Bethel. The altar’s \add four\add* projecting corners will be cut off and fall to the ground.
\v 15 I’ll destroy the winter house along with the summer house. The houses of ivory will perish, and the large houses will vanish.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.”
\c 4
\p
\v 1 Listen to this message, you \add women like the\add* cows of Bashan on Samaria’s mountain—
\q1 you who oppress the poor,
\q1 you who crush the needy,
\q1 you who say to your husbands,
\q1 “Bring us drinks.”
\p
\v 2 My master Yahweh has sworn by his \add own\add* sinlessness saying,
\q1 “Listen, the time will come
\q1 when they’ll take you all away with hooks—
\q1 the last of you with fish-hooks.
\q1
\v 3 You’ll all be taken straight out through breaks in the \add city\add* wall,
\q1 and you’ll be thrown out toward Harmon.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.”
\s1 The arguing of Israelites
\s4 Northern kingdom
\q1
\v 4 “Go to \add the worship centre at\add* Bethel and sin,
\q1 to \add the one at\add* Gilgal and multiply sin.
\q1 Bring your sacrifices every morning,
\q1 your one-tenth contributions every three days.
\q1
\v 5 Offer a thanksgiving sacrifice with bread.
\q1 Declare freewill offerings.
\q1 Announce them,
\q1 because that pleases you, you Israelites.
\mi That’s my master Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 6 I also gave you empty stomachs in all your cities
\q1 and lack of bread in all your places.
\q1 ^Yet you all haven’t returned to me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 7 I also withheld rain from you
\q1 when there were still three months to the harvest.
\q1 I made it rain on one city,
\q1 and didn’t send rain to some another city.
\q1 One piece of land was rained on,
\q1 but the piece of land where he didn’t send rain, dried up.
\q1
\v 8 \add People from\add* two or three cities staggered to another city to drink water,
\q1 ^but \add still\add* weren’t able to get enough.
\q1 ^Yet you all haven’t returned to me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 9 I ruined your crops with blight and mildew.
\q1 The multitude of your gardens and your vineyards,
\q1 your fig trees and your olive trees,
\q1 were all devastated by the locusts.
\q1 ^Yet you all haven’t returned to me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 10 I sent deadly diseases onto you similar to what I did in Egypt.
\q1 I killed your young men with the sword, carried away your horses,
\q1 and made the stench of your camp reach your noses.
\q1 ^Yet you all haven’t returned to me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 11 I overthrew \add cities\add* among you, \add just\add* like when God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah.
\q1 You were like a burning stick snatched out of the fire.
\q1 ^Yet you all haven’t returned to me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 12 Therefore I’ll do something terrible to you, Israel,
\q1 \add and\add* because I’ll do something terrible to you,
\q1 prepare to meet your god, Israel.
\q1
\v 13 Now listen. He who formed the mountains and created the wind
\q1 reveals his thoughts to humanity,
\q1 makes the morning darkness,
\q1 and walks around the highest places in this world.
\q1 Commander-in-chief God, Yahweh, is his name.”
\c 5
\s1 Encouraging repentance from sin
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Hear this message of mourning over you that I take up, people of Israel:
\q1
\v 2 The virgin Israel has fallen—she won’t rise again.
\q1 She’s abandoned on her land—there’s no one to help her up,
\q1
\v 3 because this is what the master Yahweh says:
\q1 “The city that went out with a thousand \add warriors\add* will have a hundred left,
\q1 and the one that went out with a hundred will have ten left belonging to the nation of Israel.”
\p
\v 4 This is what Yahweh says to the people of Israel:
\q1 “Seek me and live!
\q1
\v 5 Don’t seek Bethel \add which means ‘God’s house’)\add*
\q1 or enter Gilgal;
\q1 Don’t journey \add down\add* to Beersheba.
\q1 Because Gilgal will surely go into captivity,
\q1 and Bethel will become nothing.
\p
\v 6 Seek Yahweh and live,
\q1 or he will break out like fire among Joseph’s descendants.
\q1 It will devour, and there’ll be no one to quench it in Bethel.
\q1
\v 7 Those people turn justice into a bitter thing
\q1 and throw righteousness down to the ground.”
\p
\v 8 God made the Pleiades and Orion \add constellations\add*.
\q1 He turns darkness into the morning
\q1 and he makes the day dark with night
\q1 He calls for the waters of the sea
\q1 and pours them out on the surface of the earth.
\q1 Yahweh is his name.
\q1
\v 9 He causes devastation to land on the strong,
\q1 and destruction comes down on the fortresses.
\q1
\v 10 They hate anyone who corrects them at the community centre,
\q1 and they despise anyone who speaks the truth.
\q1
\v 11 Therefore, because you trample down the poor
\q1 and take a percentage of \add all\add* their grain—
\q1 although you have built houses from nice stone blocks, you won’t \add get to\add* live in them.
\q1 You have delightful vineyards, but you won’t drink their wine.
\q1
\v 12 I know how many your offenses are
\q1 and how great your sins are—
\q1 you who make honest people suffer, take bribes,
\q1 and turn aside the needy at the community centre.
\q1
\v 13 Therefore any sensible person should be silent at such a time,
\q1 because it’s an evil time.
\p
\v 14 Strive to do good and not evil, so that you may live.
\q1 So Yahweh, the god with armies, will really be with you,
\q1 just like you’ve all been saying.
\q1
\v 15 Hate evil, love good, and establish justice at the community centre.
\q1 Perhaps Yahweh, the god with armies, will be gracious to the remnant of Joseph’s \add descendants\add*.
\p
\v 16 Therefore, this is what Yahweh says, the god with armies, my master,
\q1 “Wailing will be in all the \add town\add* squares,
\q1 and in all the streets they’ll complain, ‘Oh no. Oh no.’
\q1 They’ll call the farmers to mourning
\q1 and the mourners to wail.
\v 17 There’ll be wailing in all the vineyards because I’ll pass through among you,”
\mi Yahweh says.
\s1 Warning about coming judgement
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 18 You who desire the day of Yahweh won’t end well.
\q1 Why do you long for the day of Yahweh?
\q1 It’ll be darkness and not light,
\q1
\v 19 like when a person flees from a lion and then they run into a bear,
\q1 or when a person goes in a house and puts a hand on the wall and gets bitten by a snake.
\q1
\v 20 Won’t Yahweh’s day consist of darkness and not light?
\q1 Gloom and no brightness?
\q1
\v 21 “I hate, I despise your festivals,
\q1 I take no delight in your formal gatherings.
\q1
\v 22 Even though you all offer me your burnt offerings and grain offerings, I won’t accept them,
\q1 nor will I take any notice of your fellowship offerings of fattened animals.
\q1
\v 23 Take the noise of your songs away from me.
\q1 I won’t listen to the sound of your harps.
\q1
\v 24 Instead, let justice flow like water,
\q1 and righteousness like a constantly flowing stream.
\p
\v 25 You descendants of Israel,
\q1 did you bring me sacrifices and offerings in the wilderness for forty years?
\q1
\v 26 You’ve all lifted up the images of Sikut, your king,
\q1 and Kiyun, your star god that you made for yourselves.
\v 27 Therefore I will exile you \add far\add* beyond Damascus,”
\mi says Yahweh, whose name is the god with armies.
\c 6
\s1 Exile for the complacent
\s4 Northern kingdom
\q1
\v 1 Those who are at ease in Zion won’t end well,
\q1 nor will those who feel secure in the Samaria hill country—
\q1 the notable men of the best of the nations—
\q1 the ones who Israel’s descendants come to for help!
\q1
\v 2 “Go to Keleneh and look.
\q1 From there go to Hamat-Rabah.
\q1 Then go down to the Philistine \add city of\add* Gath.
\q1 Are they better than your two kingdoms?
\q1 Is their territory larger than yours?”
\q1
\v 3 Those who refuse to consider the day of disaster won’t end well
\q1 and make the seat of violence come closer.
\q1
\v 4 They lie on ivory beds
\q1 and lounge on their couches.
\q1 They eat lambs from the flock
\q1 and calves from the stall.
\q1
\v 5 They sing fun songs with the harp as accompaniment.
\q1 They improvise on musical instruments like David did.
\q1
\v 6 They drink bowls of wine
\q1 and anoint themselves with the finest oils,
\q1 ^but they don’t grieve over Israel’s ruin.
\q1
\v 7 Therefore, they’ll go into exile now with the first exiles,
\q1 and the feasts of those who lounged about will come to an end.
\p
\v 8 This is the declaration of Yahweh, the god with armies:
\q1 “I, the master Yahweh, have sworn by myself
\q1 that I detest Israel’s pride.
\q1 I hate their fortresses,
\q1 →therefore I’ll hand the city\f + \fr 6:8 \ft Probably Samaria.\f* over with everything that’s in it.”
\p
\v 9 When that happens, if there’s ten people left in a house, they’ll all die.
\v 10 When a person’s relative comes to take their bodies out of the house to be burnt outside, if he asks the person in the house if there’s anyone with them and if that person says there’s not, then he’ll say, “Don’t say anything else because we mustn’t mention Yahweh’s name.”
\q1
\v 11 \add Yes,\add* because, listen, Yahweh will give a command,
\q1 and the big house will be smashed to pieces,
\q1 and the little house \add turned\add* into rubble.
\q1
\v 12 Do horses run on the rocky cliffs?
\q1 Would someone plough there with oxen?
\q1 Yet you’ve all turned justice into poison
\q1 and the results of doing what’s right, into bitterness,
\q1
\v 13 you who celebrate about \add capturing\add* Lo-Devar—
\q1 who say, “Didn’t we take Karnayim by our own strength?”
\q1
\v 14 Therefore, listen, I’ll raise up a nation against you people of Israel.
\mi That’s the declaration of Yahweh, the god with armies.
\q1 They’ll afflict you
\q1 from Levo-Hamat to the brook in the Aravah.”
\c 7
\s1 The locusts vision
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is what my master Yahweh showed me:
\m Listen, he formed a locust swarm when the spring crop began to come up, and oh no, it was the late crop after the king’s \add initial\add* harvest.
\v 2 When they’d finished eating everything green across the land, then I asked, “My master Yahweh, please forgive me. How will Israel survive because it’s only a small \add country\add*?”
\p
\v 3 \add So\add* Yahweh backed off from that and said that he wouldn’t do it.
\s1 The wildfire vision
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 4 This is what my master Yahweh showed me:
\m Listen, my master Yahweh called on fire to judge the country. It dried up the vast, deep water under the earth and \add also would have\add* devoured the land
\v 5 except I said, “My master Yahweh, please stop. How will Israel survive because it’s so small?”
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* Yahweh also backed off from that and said that he wouldn’t do it.
\s1 The plumb-line vision
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 7 This is what he showed me:
\m Listen, my master stood on a wall holding a plumb-line
\v 8 and he asked me, “Amos, what do you see?”
\p “A plumb line,” I answered.
\p “Listen, I’ll drop a plumb-line \add down to check out\add* my people Israel,” my master said. “Never again will I spare them.
\v 9 The hilltop shrines of Isaac will be destroyed.
\q1 Israel’s sanctuaries will be ruined,
\q1 and I’ll take action against the house of Jeroboam with the sword.”
\s1 Amos and Amasiyas
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 10 Then Amatsyah, the priest at Bethel, sent a message to Israel’s King Jeroboam, saying: “Amos has conspired against you right among the Israelite people. The country can’t handle all his messages.
\v 11 Amos is saying that \add you,\add* Jeroboam, will die by the sword and that Israel will definitely get taken into exile away from this land.”
\p
\v 12 Amatsyah ordered Amos, “Hey prophet! Go \add and\add* run yourself back to the Judah region, and earn your living and prophesy there.
\v 13 But you must never again prophesy here at Bethel because it’s the king’s sanctuary, and it’s the royal residence.”
\p
\v 14 “I’m not a prophet,” Amos answered the priest. “Nor am I a prophet’s son, because I’m a herdsman, plus I take care of an orchard.
\v 15 But Yahweh took me from looking after the flock and told me, ‘Go, prophesy to my people Israel.’
\v 16 So now listen to Yahweh’s message: You say, ‘Don’t prophesy against Israel, and don’t speak out against Isaac’s descendants.’
\v 17 Therefore this is what Yahweh says:
\q1 Your wife will become a prostitute in the city.
\q1 Your sons and daughters will die by the sword.
\q1 Your property will be measured and divided up.
\q1 You’ll die in a foreign, heathen country,
\q1 and Israel will certainly be taken into exile from its land.”
\c 8
\s1 The basket-of-fruit vision
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is what my master Yahweh showed me: See this basketful of summer fruit.
\v 2 Then he asked, “What do you see, Amos?”
\p “A basket of summer fruit,” I said.
\p Then Yahweh told me, “The end has come for my people Israel. I won’t spare them any longer.
\v 3 The songs of the temple will become wailings on that day.”
\mi That’s the declaration of my master Yahweh.
\q1 “Many dead bodies—they’re thrown everywhere.
\q1 \add Then\add* silence.”
\s1 Israel’s punishment
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 4 Listen to this, you who trample the needy
\q1 and take the poor off the land.
\v 5 They say,
\q1 “When will the new moon \add celebrations\add* be over, so we can sell grain \add again\add*?
\q1 When will the Rest Day be over, so we can sell wheat?
\q1 We’ll make the measure small and increase the price,
\q1 as we cheat with false scales.
\q1
\v 6 This is so we can sell low-grade wheat.
\q1 We can buy the poor people with cash,
\q1 and the needy people for a pair of sandals.”
\p
\v 7 Yahweh has made an oath on Jacob’s pride,
\q1 “I certainly won’t ever forget any of their \add evil\add* actions.
\q1
\v 8 Won’t the land \add itself\add* quake due to this \add behaviour\add*,
\q1 and every one who lives in it mourn?
\q1 All of the ground will come up like the Nile river \add after the rains\add*,
\q1 and it will be tossed about then settle again, like Egypt’s river.
\p
\v 9 This is the declaration of my master Yahweh:
\q1 “It will come at that time that I’ll make the sun set at noon,
\q1 and I will darken the earth during the day.
\q1
\v 10 I’ll turn your festivals into mourning
\q1 and all your songs into dirges.
\q1 I’ll make all of you wear \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth
\q1 and every head to be shaved.
\q1 I’ll make it like mourning for an only son,
\q1 and a bitter day to its end.
\p
\v 11 This is the declaration of my master Yahweh:
\q1 Listen, the days are coming when I’ll send a famine to this country.
\q1 Not a famine for bread or a thirst for water,
\q1 ^but for hearing Yahweh’s messages.
\q1
\v 12 They’ll stagger from sea to sea.
\q1 They’ll rove around from the north to the east to seek Yahweh’s message,
\q1 ^but they won’t find it.
\q1
\v 13 In that day the beautiful young women
\q1 and the young men will faint from dehydration.
\q1
\v 14 Those who swear by the guilt of Samaria
\q1 and say, ‘As your god lives, Dan,’
\q1 and, ‘As the road to Beersheba exists’—
\q1 they will die and never live again.”
\c 9
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement on Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 I saw my master standing beside the altar, and he said,
\q1 “Strike the tops of the pillars so that the foundations will shake.
\q1 Break them in pieces onto all their heads,
\q1 and I will kill the last of them with the sword.
\q1 Not one of them will get away.
\q1 Not one of them will escape.
\q1
\v 2 Though they dig \add down\add* towards Sheol, my hand will reach in there and pluck them out.
\q1 Though they climb up to heaven, I’ll bring them \add back\add* down from there.
\q1
\v 3 Even if they hide on the top of \add Mt.\add* Carmel, I’ll search there and take them.
\q1 Though they’re hidden from my sight at the bottom of the sea, I’ll give orders to the serpent there to bite them.
\q1
\v 4 Though they go into captivity, driven ahead by their enemies, I’ll give orders there to the sword to kill them.
\q1 I’ll keep my eyes on them for harm and not for good.”
\q1
\v 5 Master \add and\add* Commander-in-chief Yahweh touches the land and it melts.
\q1 All who live in it mourn.
\q1 All of it will rise up like the Nile river,
\q1 then go down again like Egypt’s river.
\q1
\v 6 He builds his steps in the heavens,
\q1 and has established his vault over the earth.
\q1 He calls for the waters of the sea,
\q1 and pours them out on the earth’s surface,
\q1 Yahweh is his name.
\p
\v 7 This is Yahweh’s declaration:
\q1 “Aren’t you all like Ethiopia’s sons to me, you Israelites?
\q1 Didn’t I bring Israel out of Egypt,
\q1 the Philistines from Crete,
\q1 and the Arameans from Kir?
\q1
\v 8 Listen, the eyes of my master Yahweh are on the sinful kingdom \add of Israel\add*,
\q1 and I will destroy it from the face of the earth,
\q1 except that I won’t totally wipe out Jacob’s descendants.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 9 Because, listen, I will give a command,
\q1 and I’ll shake Israel among all the nations,
\q1 as one shakes grain in a sieve,
\q1 but not even a pebble will fall to the ground.
\v 10 All my people who are sinners will die by the sword,
\q1 \add including\add* those who said, ‘We’re totally immune from any disaster.’
\s1 Israel’s future restoration
\s4 Full kingdom
\q1
\v 11 At that time, I’ll raise up David’s shelter that collapsed, and repair where it’s split.
\q1 I’ll raise up its ruins, and rebuild it as in the days of old.
\q1
\v 12 Consequently they may take over what’s left of Edom
\q1 and all the nations that have decided to follow me.
\mi That is Yahweh’s declaration—he’ll do it.
\p
\v 13 This is Yahweh’s declaration:
\q1 Listen, the days will come when the man on the plough will overtake the harvester,
\q1 and the treader of grapes will overtake the one planting seed.
\q1 The mountains will drip sweet wine,
\q1 and all the hills will flow with it.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll bring my people Israel back from captivity.
\q1 They’ll build the ruined cities and inhabit them.
\q1 They’ll plant vineyards and drink their wine,
\q1 and they’ll make gardens and eat their fruit.
\q1
\v 15 I’ll plant them onto their land,
\q1 and they’ll never again be uprooted from the land that I’ve given them,”
\q1 says your god Yahweh.
